<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Friedrich Nietzsche</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1844">1844</a>–<a href="/years/1900">1900</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Friedrich Wilhelm Nietzsche, Nietzsche</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Friedrich Nietzsche was a German philosopher and writer who began as a classical philologist and in 1869, at 24, became Professor of Classical Philology at the University of Basel. Ill health made him resign in 1879, and he lived as an independent writer in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His philosophy includes a critique of objective truth, a genealogical critique of Christian morality, and the concepts of the will to power, the Übermensch and eternal return. After his collapse in 1889 his sister, Elisabeth Förster-Nietzsche, edited his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> to fit her ultranationalist ideology, and his work became associated with fascism and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazism">Nazism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/friedrich-nietzsche.jpg" alt="Friedrich Nietzsche" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Friedrich Hermann Hartmann · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nietzsche187a.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the modern West the prophet’s name was given to <b>Friedrich Nietzsche</b>’s philosophical book Thus Spoke Zarathustra, published in the 1880s, though its ideas are Nietzsche’s own.</blockquote><a href="/religion/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Schopenhauer questioned earlier optimism, and Søren Kierkegaard and <b>Friedrich Nietzsche</b> rejected the idea that reality could be understood purely objectively.</blockquote><a href="/movement/modernism">Modernism · Background</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Friedrich Nietzsche</b> called its claim the most idiotic slogan in the world, and Kurt Tucholsky named a satirical photo book of 1929 after it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/deutschlandlied">Deutschlandlied</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Man is a rope stretched between the animal and the Superman—a rope over an abyss.&#8221;</p><footer>Thus Spake Zarathustra (1883), Prologue &#183; tr. Thomas Common, 1909 &#183; spoken by Zarathustra &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Thus_Spake_Zarathustra/Prologue" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;I tell you: one must still have chaos in one, to give birth to a dancing star. I tell you: ye have still chaos in you.&#8221;</p><footer>Thus Spake Zarathustra (1883), Prologue &#183; tr. Thomas Common, 1909 &#183; spoken by Zarathustra &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Thus_Spake_Zarathustra/Prologue" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;That which does not kill me, makes me stronger.&#8221;</p><footer>The Twilight of the Idols (1889), &quot;Maxims and Arrows&quot; &#183; tr. Anthony M. Ludovici, 1911 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/52263/pg52263-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>1 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;What trifles constitute happiness! The sound of a bagpipe. Without music life would be a mistake.&#8221;</p><footer>The Twilight of the Idols (1889), &quot;Maxims and Arrows&quot; &#183; tr. Anthony M. Ludovici, 1911 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/52263/pg52263-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
